    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7b56c68319c1cdc4400f259f24e9097e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1759,
    "chunk_text": "China appears to be losing, one by one, its most reliable partners, exposing vulnerabilities in Beijing\u2019s global network that have long been suspected but are now increasingly tangible. Amid these international upheavals, Xi Jinping presided over the Two Sessions in Beijing, which had just begun at the time of writing and will continue until 11 March.\n\nThis concentration of authority carries inherent risks, as vacant senior positions and less experienced replacements could compromise operational effectiveness, particularly regarding Taiwan, where the PLA is expected to secure a \u201cstrategic decisive victory\u201d. Taiwan observes these developments with apprehension, interpreting the purges both as a demonstration of Xi\u2019s authority and as an indication of Beijing\u2019s potential assertiveness. In Hong Kong, enforcement under the National Security Law continues to suppress civil and business activity, creating further domestic strain. Overseas, the Belt and Road Initiative faces new uncertainties. The recent Panama ruling, in which a Hong Kong operator lost control of the Panama Canal\u2014a critical artery for global trade\u2014highlights the fragility of China\u2019s overseas infrastructure projects.\n\nYet AidData at William & Mary University reports that Chinese state institutions have invested in 363 ports worldwide over the past 25 years, totaling US$23.9 billion. Nearly half of this portfolio went to high-income countries, including Australia, New Zealand, Spain, Brunei, and Singapore, reflecting an effort to secure trade networks and mitigate East-West supply chain decoupling. Even so, setbacks such as Panama reveal the limits of influence, illustrating that infrastructure alone cannot fully insulate China from geopolitical realities."
