    "md5": "0e1262d5700b51a37ba1e395aff24410",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1897,
    "chunk_text": "The forest gained national attention in 2021 when environmental groups exposed extensive illegal clearing carried out in parts of the reserve. Large areas had been cut down primarily for cultivation and land encroachment, sparking protests from conservationists who warned that the destruction would have long-term ecological consequences.\n\nAccording to Withanage, the number of trees cut during the illegal operation was estimated at around 8,000, a figure that illustrates the scale of the environmental damage inflicted within a relatively short period.\n\n\u201cThe destruction happened very quickly,\u201d he said. \u201cBut restoring a forest ecosystem takes decades. A dry-zone forest cannot recover overnight.\u201d\n\nDespite the devastation, environmentalists say the forest is now showing encouraging signs of natural recovery.\n\nYoung saplings, shrubs and grasses have begun to grow in areas that were once stripped bare. Some patches are slowly developing secondary forest cover as nature attempts to reclaim the damaged landscape.\n\n\u201cEarly regeneration is a positive sign,\u201d Withanage explained. \u201cBut the ecosystem is still in a very delicate state. If further disturbances occur now, the recovery process could be reversed.\u201d\n\nOne of the most alarming new threats to the recovering forest is the rapid increase in illegal cattle grazing within the Kebiliththa area.\n\nEnvironmentalists say herds of cattle are being brought into the forest by livestock owners who allow the animals to roam freely in the regenerating vegetation.\n\nThis practice, they warn, is severely undermining the forest\u2019s natural recovery.\n\n\u201cWith the forest beginning to regenerate, cattle grazing has become a serious issue,\u201d Withanage said. \u201cLarge numbers of cattle are now being brought into the area. These animals feed on young saplings and destroy new vegetation that is essential for the forest to recover.\u201d"
  },
