    "md5": "1bffea808fc99d835611e692c2f88a8d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1936,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cWith the forest beginning to regenerate, cattle grazing has become a serious issue,\u201d Withanage said. \u201cLarge numbers of cattle are now being brought into the area. These animals feed on young saplings and destroy new vegetation that is essential for the forest to recover.\u201d\n\nOver time, such disturbances can prevent forests from re-establishing their natural structure.\n\n\u201cIf cattle continue to graze freely in these recovering areas, the forest will struggle to regenerate properly,\u201d Withanage warned.\n\nEnvironmentalists also point out that cattle grazing inside protected forests can have broader ecological consequences.\n\nIn addition to damaging vegetation, large numbers of livestock can introduce invasive plant species, degrade soil quality and disrupt wildlife habitats.\n\nThere are also concerns that cattle presence in wildlife habitats could increase the risk of disease transmission between livestock and wild animals.\n\nMoreover, grazing inside forests frequently leads to conflicts with wildlife, particularly elephants.\n\nKebiliththa lies within an important elephant habitat zone in southeastern Sri Lanka. The forests surrounding the region serve as critical feeding grounds and movement corridors for elephant populations.\n\nWhen cattle are brought into these forests, they compete directly with wild herbivores for food and space.\n\nEnvironmentalists warn that such competition could further intensify Sri Lanka\u2019s already severe human\u2013elephant conflict.\n\n\u201cElephants depend on these forests for food,\u201d Withanage said. \u201cWhen humans bring cattle into the same areas, it creates additional pressure on the ecosystem and increases the chances of conflict.\u201d\n\nSri Lanka has been grappling with one of the world\u2019s most serious human-elephant conflict crises. Each year, hundreds of elephants and dozens of people lose their lives in confrontations that are largely driven by habitat loss and fragmentation."
