    "md5": "490e781ebbba3c4975e57a0d26b49fdd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1990,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka has been grappling with one of the world\u2019s most serious human-elephant conflict crises. Each year, hundreds of elephants and dozens of people lose their lives in confrontations that are largely driven by habitat loss and fragmentation.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s forest cover has been steadily declining over the decades due to agricultural expansion, infrastructure development and illegal encroachment.\n\nAlthough official figures often highlight improvements in overall tree cover, environmental scientists note that natural forests \u2014 which contain complex ecosystems and biodiversity \u2014 are continuing to shrink.\n\nDry-zone forests like those in Monaragala play an especially important role in maintaining ecological balance.\n\nThey act as natural water catchments, store carbon, prevent soil erosion and provide habitat for countless plant and animal species.\n\nWhen such forests are cleared or degraded, the environmental consequences can extend far beyond the immediate area.\n\nFor this reason, environmentalists say the recovery of Kebiliththa must be protected through strict enforcement of conservation laws.\n\nAuthorities must ensure that illegal clearing does not resume and that activities such as cattle grazing inside the forest are properly controlled.\n\n\u201cStopping deforestation was only the first step,\u201d Withanage said. \u201cNow we must protect the forest while it is recovering. Otherwise the damage that has already been done will only worsen.\u201d\n\nEnvironmental groups have repeatedly called for stronger monitoring of vulnerable forest reserves across Sri Lanka.\n\nThey argue that many illegal activities continue largely because enforcement agencies lack adequate resources and coordination.\n\nThe case of Kebiliththa, they say, should serve as a national lesson on the importance of protecting natural ecosystems before irreversible damage occurs.\n\nDespite the challenges, the gradual regrowth now visible in parts of the forest offers a reminder of nature\u2019s resilience."
