    "chunk_size_bytes": 1801,
    "chunk_text": "For the most part he is content to let his characters speak for themselves, and has selected extracts from diaries that bring out their individuality. As we saw with the Thai prince, he notes the affection between father and son, and he deals with a similar subject in describing the worries Mrs Bradman felt about her husband. Oddly enough, what emerges most strongly from the account of Bradman is how sick he was so often, on the ship when he first came over, and in Ceylon too, so that once he was unable to take the field, to the chagrin of his enthusiastic supporters in Colombo.\n\nAnd Karunakaran does make the occasional very pertinent observation. When Roerich writes about a port full of Europeans \u2013 \u2018The policeman is Irish. A French peddler. A Greek with postcards. Dutch tea-peddlers. An Italian chauffeur. But where are the Singhalese? Have they all emigrated to Europe?\u2019 \u2013 Karunakaran questions the \u2018widely believed narratives of all Europeans on the island living in affluence while working-class locals struggled to make ends meet\u2019.\n\nHe is harsh about what he sees as Carpenter\u2019s racism, and asks how \u2018the daughter of someone who supported the abolition of slavery in the United States\u2019 could harbour such deep racial prejudice? But even she is more positive than otherwise about the beauties of the island, and also of its children, while he records the ecstasy of others, in particular Mark Twain who said he had spent the most enchanting day of his life in Ceylon, and loved everything \u2013 except the prim European clothes of girls marching out of a missionary school. He much preferred the boy with just a twine string round his waist, which he thought \u2018a pleasant contrast with the odious flummery in which the little Sunday-school dowdies were masquerading\u2019."
  },
